      Update tree-sitter grammars: bash (v0.25.1) (#7223)
      
      Automated grammar update for **bash**: `tree-sitter-bash` `30d369ba` →
      [`a06c2e44`](https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-bash/tree/a06c2e4415e9bc0346c6b86d401879ffb44058f7)
      (**v0.25.1**), tree-sitter generator `0.22.6` → `0.26.3`, and the bash
      scanner is now C (`scanner.c`, was `scanner.cc`). Parser and CST files
      are regenerated.
      
      ### User-facing changes
      
      - Arguments after a file redirect or heredoc delimiter stay with their
      command (`echo a > out b`, `cat <<EOF a b`).
      - `until` loops parse as (negated) while loops instead of separate
      commands.
      - Arithmetic (`$(( ))`, `$[ ]`, `(( ))`, C-style `for` headers) is
      translated into operator trees with bash precedence and associativity,
      and accepts `...` and `<... ...>` in patterns. It is no longer confused
      with `$(...)`.
      - `#` inside words and numbers (`x=foo#bar`, `2#101`) no longer
      truncates them.
      - ``$`cmd` `` is a literal `$` plus a backquote substitution; negated
      assignments (`! x=1`) keep the `!`; C-style `for` headers are modeled.
      - Constructs that failed to parse now parse: `$[ ]`, arithmetic
      subscripts, bases and ternaries, `if` function bodies, `time ( ... )`,
      extglob in `case` and `[[ ]]`, special-character heredoc delimiters, fd
      here-strings, single-line case items.
      - Patterns can end a block with a lone `...`: `while ...; do ... done`
      and `if ...; then ... fi` now parse (they were a syntax error, also at
      the previous pin;
      [CODE-10062](https://linear.app/semgrep/issue/CODE-10062/bash-allow-a-trailing-before-donefi-in-patterns-eg-while-do-done)).
      
      **Build system change**
      `flags.sh` now links `libunwind.a` and `libstdc++.a` as a group.
      `forge-image` had an implicit dependency on the grammars' C++ standard
      library binding. By removing the last `scanner.cc`, the static link
      `forge-image` failed with `multiple definition of '_Unwind_Resume'`.
      
      
      **Wrapper changes** (`semgrep-bash/grammar.js`):
      
      - redirected statements exclude pipelines (works around upstream
      [#347](https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-bash/issues/347))
      - `function_definition` synced;
      - a lone `...` may end a block without a terminator (a dedicated `'...'`
      token, accepted wherever a word is). Any other command still needs one,
      otherwise `done` would be lexed as a keyword in `for i in a; do echo
      done; done`;
      - upstream arithmetic precedence fixed (unary binds tighter than binary,
      `**`, assignments and `?:` are right-associative);
      - `prep` patches one inherited corpus expectation (`export $FOO:$BAR`)
      so `declare -r $VAR` patterns keep working.
      
      **Limitations**
      - **New in this PR (upstream v0.25.1 CST):** a heredoc followed by a
      pipe and then `&&` or `||` is grouped as `cat | (grep x && echo ok)`
      instead of `(cat | grep x) && echo ok`, so patterns that depend on pipe
      versus `&&`/`||` structure can give wrong results. The previous pin
      grouped it correctly. Plain `cat <<EOF | grep x` is unaffected.
      Reproducer: `printf 'cat <<EOF | grep x && echo ok\nbody\nEOF\n' > t.sh;
      semgrep-core -lang bash -dump_ast t.sh` shows `Pipe(cat, And(grep x,
      echo ok))`. Not fixed here: it is an upstream parsing bug.
      - A command literally named `...` followed directly by a keyword (`for i
      in a; do ... done; done`) now closes the block early. Other uses of
      `...` as an argument (`do foo ... done`) are invalid bash, so they stay
      syntax errors.
      - **Pre-existing:** `[ ]`/`[[ ]]` tests and `${...}` with a subscript or
      `${#!}` stay `BashTodo`; heredoc bodies are not in the generic AST;
      `$X=$X` does not match `a=$a`.
      - **Pre-existing:** parameter-expansion operators (`${x#pat}`,
      `${x:-d}`, ...) collapse to the variable, so `echo ${I}` also matches
      `echo ${I#*;}`.
      
      
      **Validation:** harness gate passed; `make -C OSS test-grammar-bash`
      (131 corpus cases); OSS bash tests 142/142, Dockerfile 130/130; all 82
      upstream corpus inputs parse and translate. The full OSS suite had one
      failure, a Terraform parse error in `semgrep-rules`
      (`keyvault-ensure-secret-expires.tf`) in code this PR does not touch.
      For the `...` change, old and new grammars were compared on 259 bash
      test files and about 45 probe snippets: parse trees are identical except
      for the newly accepted patterns.
      
